</node>
<node id="Louis XIV">
  <data key="d0">Louis XIV</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Louis XIV is a historical figure associated with a war period where infantry usage patterns shifted significantly.&lt;SEP&gt;Louis XIV's era saw the cessation of large-scale infantry usage as wars became based on money, recruiting, and mercenaries rather than feudal obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Louis XIV was the monarch during whose reign external wars occurred, characterized by maneuver-based warfare in early periods and increased intensity later.&lt;SEP&gt;Louis XIV marks a historical period from which modern warfare scale evolution begins.&lt;SEP&gt;Louis XIV was a King of France whose reign established the context for the French Old Regime and the Five-Day Travel System.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774689</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thirty Years' War">
  <data key="d0">Thirty Years' War</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Thirty Years' War marks a period when warfare was established on financial and recruitment bases, reducing large-scale infantry usage initially.&lt;SEP&gt;The Thirty Years' War was a conflict between Protestant princes, Catholic powers, and the Imperial emperor starting in 1618.&lt;SEP&gt;The Thirty Years' War was a major European conflict spanning from 1618 to 1648 involving major continental powers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="War of Louis XIV">
  <data key="d0">War of Louis XIV</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">This war period followed the Thirty Years' War and saw the maintenance of infantry advantage through firearm improvements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infantry">
  <data key="d0">Infantry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infantry is a fundamental military branch composed of foot soldiers who form the bulk of many armies, particularly utilized in People's Wars due to equipment shortages. Its prominence grew through firearm improvements, with numerical strength increasing significantly during the 17th and 18th centuries. Infantry engages in warfare using both destructive firepower and close-quarters bayonet combat, offering superior versatility and the capacity for both defense and attack. Among the three primary branches, it possesses the strongest independent combat capability and maintains clear superiority over other troops in difficult terrain.

Strategically, Infantry is identified as the primary military branch with other forces subordinate to its role, though it requires close coordination with mobile assets to avoid delays in operational execution. Within the army structure, it may be required to provide protection for artillery moving along main roads, especially when cavalry resources are reallocated to this branch. Historical analyses compare infantry formations to cavalry based on establishment costs and ratios. In terms of specific formations, infantry constitutes the central columns in the Old Battle Sequence marching formations and formed the middle routes of four-route deployments in old sequences. Consequently, the branch is analyzed regarding its numerical strength and tactical importance driven by these firearm advancements and structural necessities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68695250ec43f3b423a5df5d30602c2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58e69b3684e2bdfaee22cb77833e8174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38f13e59336c7bb333fab6bbbe0ac097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a0950d107000f63848660e8ffc0706</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artillery">
  <data key="d0">Artillery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Artillery functions as a military branch recognized primarily for enhancing firepower through destructive capabilities in battlefield engagements. While described as a powerful component increasingly essential for eliminating enemies, it relies heavily on this destructive firepower and possesses no independent combat capability when operating alone. Consequently, Artillery is a critical combined arms component forming modern battle formations alongside infantry and cavalry, having been utilized alongside cavalry in European military configurations of the past. Historically, these heavy weapons were utilized by weaker armies to balance power against stronger forces, notably by Frederick the Great. However, as weapon systems reliant on firepower and space, Artillery faces disadvantages in dense forests or uneven terrain, requiring large main roads to maintain effectiveness.

In terms of mobility and logistics, Artillery units present challenges regarding movement and protection needs, often requiring large main roads and support from infantry during marches. To ensure safer and easier movement, gun units may march separately from the main force along safe roads, yet this creates a dependency on significant logistical support that can become burdensome during supply crises. The risks inherent in these logistical and positional demands are highlighted by historical records noting that around 30 artillery pieces were lost by Murat's forces during a scouting battle due to insufficient forward line extension, underscoring the vulnerability of these units when not properly integrated or protected during complex maneuvers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68695250ec43f3b423a5df5d30602c2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ceef5e0b237a9edd2580a6c4fbfed04</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774705</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Westphalia">
  <data key="d0">Westphalia</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Westphalia is a region within Prussia, mentioned alongside the state itself regarding territorial limitations.&lt;SEP&gt;Westphalia is a region located far from the home territory, covering part of the national area.&lt;SEP&gt;Westphalia was a region divided into small principalities during Frederick II's reign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774705</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Napoleon">
  <data key="d0">Napoleon</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Napoleon is recognized as the most outstanding commander of modern times, known for concentrating superior force to secure victories in most battles. As a French leader, he fought against coalitions during early 19th-century conflicts, leading the French Army through a series of retreats and battles involving both victories and defeats until his eventual defeat at the Battle of Waterloo. He commanded larger armies ranging from 100,000 to 200,000 men, frequently deploying strong vanguard units, and utilized maximum conscription alongside auxiliary troops to support massive military operations. His strategic approach included effectively utilizing cavalry superiority when the operational area was vast and large decisive strikes were intended, with his force composition often discussed regarding cavalry and infantry ratios.

Regarding specific campaigns, Napoleon led the French Central Army during the 1812 invasion of Russia, where he attempted to cut off Russian army lines. He stayed near Vitebsk in 1812 and delayed attacks due to fear of facing multiple enemies, yet the retreat plan was severely constrained by supply issues during the 1812 Russian Campaign, which suffered losses due to inadequate supply management. Consequently, Napoleon decided to withdraw from Russia earlier due to the failure at the Battle of Tarutino. While wars under his command utilized a mix of supply methods situated between two extremes, he emphasized the importance of logistics through his campaigns in Spain and Russia. Historically, he is compared to Frederick the Great, noted for potentially having better provisions for his army. During the 1815 campaign against the Prussian forces, he commanded approximately 120,000 men, occupied Charleroi, and led reserve forces against Prussian troops. Previously, in 1806, he marched from the Rhine to the Vistula River and pursued the Prussian army with the intent to cut off retreat routes. After the defeat in the 1812 campaign, his retreat was temporarily commanded by Murat, and he had also led military advances from Northern Italy to Austria.

Historically, Napoleon is a historical antagonist referenced in opposition by Prussian generals. He is cited as an example of a commander who left a legacy defined by his massive operations and tactical decisions. Within his personal interactions, he referred to Marshal Ney as the "bravest of the brave." Ultimately, he remains a historical figure whose career spanned conflicts against coalition forces, characterized by his high-risk strategies, vast logistical demands, and enduring reputation in military history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f1e8fe38b90ff6dffdb7380ea9e159d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc6725d3545cdb40e1dfdd2209aba40f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9b9839be4f5c76a97ace71baf8a2625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68bce0e3733aefaac111940411ed6dd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ceef5e0b237a9edd2580a6c4fbfed04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774727</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cavalry">
  <data key="d0">Cavalry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cavalry refers to a military branch mounted on horses, specializing in mobile offense and individual combat rather than defensive static holding. It is essential for improving the motion capability and range of military operations, describing itself as suitable for mobility and large-scale decisive battles where it was historically considered a dominant force in medieval armies. Discussed regarding its changing proportion and value relative to infantry across different historical periods, the relative number of Cavalry may decrease over time due to technological changes. Distinct from artillery units referenced in troop composition, Cavalry acts as a mobile combat arm utilized to provide reinforcement to infantry, requiring efficient logistical timing. It serves as a mobile reserve component within the army structure and is frequently used to reinforce forward detachments or cover movements between main forces. Additionally, Cavalry is assigned primarily to advanced guards during pursuit operations for increased mobility.

In terms of deployment and formation, Cavalry units were initially deployed on the wings and later moved to the rear for greater flexibility. It constitutes the outer columns in the Old Battle Sequence marching formations, and in old sequences, Cavalry formed the outer routes of four-route deployments. On the army's flanks, Cavalry units frequently swap positions to share the honor of leading the right wing. During strategic deployments, Cavalry is specifically a unit type frequently required to quarter alongside main forces. Despite its tactical utility, the branch faces significant logistical constraints; Cavalry units are highly dependent on feed and are more vulnerable to supply shortages than infantry. Due to high consumption requirements, Cavalry units were considered a burden in large armies when food supplies were insufficient. Additionally, Cavalry refers to mounted troops which are inconvenient to use in hard-to-passage areas like mountainous or forest regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68695250ec43f3b423a5df5d30602c2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58e69b3684e2bdfaee22cb77833e8174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57f8d4f60b7c38f053668dcb48030cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d696492803499031b7e887c1814ba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38f13e59336c7bb333fab6bbbe0ac097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a0950d107000f63848660e8ffc0706</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774730</data>
