</node>
<node id="Flank Vulnerabilities">
  <data key="d0">Flank Vulnerabilities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flank Vulnerabilities represent the weakened state of the flanks that occurs when the enemy holds superior strength or better logistical positions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e42e0d19c01d89b60bcea1e8c8214463</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774740</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combat Power Factors">
  <data key="d0">Combat Power Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combat Power Factors include absolute force, relative force, troop types, and terrain which collectively determine the feasibility and range of separated deployments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e42e0d19c01d89b60bcea1e8c8214463</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-Day Forced March">
  <data key="d0">One-Day Forced March</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">One-Day Forced March defines the maximum distance a Vanguard Force can retreat from the Main Body without risking independent engagement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e42e0d19c01d89b60bcea1e8c8214463</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enemy Superiority">
  <data key="d0">Enemy Superiority</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Enemy Superiority is a condition where the opposing force holds greater numbers or advantages, rendering the Main Body's flanks vulnerable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e42e0d19c01d89b60bcea1e8c8214463</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Revolutionary Leaders">
  <data key="d0">Revolutionary Leaders</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Revolutionary Leaders refers to the commanders and decision-makers during the French Revolution who prioritized rapid deployment over establishing stable supply networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Revolutionary Leaders focused on war results rather than establishing complex supply infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Military Supply System">
  <data key="d0">Military Supply System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex logistical mechanism comparable to clock gears that drives transportation teams forward.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="War System">
  <data key="d0">War System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The War System represents the comprehensive framework of logistics and combat operations existing prior to the French Revolution, known for its complexity and dependency on supply infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;An overarching military framework that collapsed when support for the supply system ceased.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="House Owners">
  <data key="d0">House Owners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">House owners and villagers provide sustenance to troops as part of the owner supply method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Authorities">
  <data key="d0">Local Authorities</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Local authorities must implement special measures when large troop groups arrive in their regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Authorities participate in Regular Requisition to ensure orderly grain surrender by the civilian population through reasonable allocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generals">
  <data key="d0">Generals</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Generals drive soldiers into battles and manage the requisitioning of necessary resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soldiers">
  <data key="d0">Soldiers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Soldiers represent the individual military personnel who suffer physical damage, illness, or death during extended marches.&lt;SEP&gt;Soldiers are deployed to battlefields and sustained through requisition and plundering.&lt;SEP&gt;Military personnel who may carry their own rations in addition to those transported by trains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6fa33c8a5d89283faf658b97761a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adige River">
  <data key="d0">Adige River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The French Army advanced from the Adige River during their operational campaigns.&lt;SEP&gt;The Adige River is a reference point for Napoleon's advancement, noted with a length of 415 kilometers in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The Adige River is located in Northern Italy and flows into the Adriatic Sea with a length of 415 kilometers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Danube River">
  <data key="d0">Danube River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The French Army pushed towards the lower reaches of the Danube River.&lt;SEP&gt;The Danube River is Europe's second-largest river, flowing through Germany, Austria, Slovakia, Hungary, and emptying into the Black Sea with a length of 2,858 kilometers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Danube River is the second largest river in Europe, flowing through multiple nations into the Black Sea.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rhine River">
  <data key="d0">Rhine River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Rhine River bordered the western side of the historical Westphalian region.&lt;SEP&gt;The Rhine River forms part of the lowland region historically known as the Netherlands.&lt;SEP&gt;The French Army originated their advances from the Rhine River.&lt;SEP&gt;The Rhine River is referenced as a starting point for Napoleon's advance into Prussian territory during conflicts in 1806.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9b9839be4f5c76a97ace71baf8a2625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vistula River">
  <data key="d0">Vistula River</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">French forces crossed the Vistula River in January 1813 following the Moscow campaign.&lt;SEP&gt;The French Army progressed to the Vistula River utilizing local supply methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vistula River is the longest river in Poland, originating from the Carpathian Mountains and flowing into the Baltic Sea with a length of 1,047 kilometers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vistula River is the longest river in Poland, originating from the Carpathian Mountains and flowing outwards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Owner Supply">
  <data key="d0">Owner Supply</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A supply method where troops gather food directly from house owners or villages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forced Requisition">
  <data key="d0">Forced Requisition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">One of four military acquisition strategies involving seizure of resources by force.&lt;SEP&gt;Forced Requisition involves direct seizure of stores by force, enabling faster acquisition when time permits no other method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regular Requisition">
  <data key="d0">Regular Requisition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A scheduled approach for military resource acquisition.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular Requisition is described as the simplest and most effective method for securing supplies, serving as the foundation of modern warfare logistics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warehouse Supply">
  <data key="d0">Warehouse Supply</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A supply method derived from established military storage facilities.&lt;SEP&gt;A supply system relying on warehouses and financial expenditure, contrasted with on-site requisitioning for inferiority in mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Warehouse Supply is a provisioning method relying on local transport and bakeries, effectively eliminating the need for a massive army baggage train.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Farm Feeds">
  <data key="d0">Farm Feeds</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Animal fodder stored by farmers that requires minimal processing for horse use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774743</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grains">
  <data key="d0">Grains</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Farmers maintain grain reserves typically lasting families eight to fourteen days, serving as a baseline for local supply estimates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Population Density Metrics">
  <data key="d0">Population Density Metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistics measuring residents per square mile used to calculate troop supply capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Large Force">
  <data key="d0">Large Force</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Refers to armies with approximately 150,000 combat personnel requiring significant logistical support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cavalry Units">
  <data key="d0">Cavalry Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Cavalry Units refer to mounted military formations mentioned in the context of campsite selection and fodder requirements during marches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transport Teams">
  <data key="d0">Transport Teams</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Transport Teams are groups dedicated to moving supplies from warehouses and bakeries to the front line units.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport teams are units within the supply system responsible for the movement and distribution of provisions driven by the logistical mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7172c419264d7047e81ab4a26a236796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Macdonald">
  <data key="d0">Macdonald</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Macdonald was a French marshal who marched through Silesia with 60,000 to 70,000 troops without deploying a vanguard unit.&lt;SEP&gt;A historical commander who met Blücher in Silesia with three army corps during the 1813 conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Macdonald commanded a force consisting of three army corps in Silesia during 1813.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57f8d4f60b7c38f053668dcb48030cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68bce0e3733aefaac111940411ed6dd7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774745</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blucher">
  <data key="d0">Blucher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Blucher commanded opposing forces in Silesia and subsequently advanced toward the Elbe River.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774745</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Russian Army">
  <data key="d0">Russian Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Russian Army is a military organization that has participated in varied historical campaigns, ranging from mid-18th-century European conflicts to the early 19th-century Napoleonic Wars. During the Seven Years' War, the Russian Army occupied East Prussia and operated in coordination with Austria. In 1760, under the direction of commanders such as Tottleben, who is also noted as Totlebin, the army advanced towards Berlin and conducted feints against the capital city.

In the context of the 1812 Campaign against French forces, the Russian Army faced significant challenges. Despite being described as inferior in organization and training compared to the enemy, it fought significant battles and employed deep strategic retreats. Initially, the force was depicted retreating before the French Army during the initial phase of the campaign, utilizing the geographical expanse of Russia as a defensive base while moving through various cities. High command included Pyotr Ivanovich Bagration and Ostermann-Tolstoi, with specific engagements occurring under Bennigsen, such as at the Battle of Tarutino. Major confrontations involved positions near Moscow. Following the enemy's advance and subsequent withdrawal, the Russian Army successfully pursued the French forces from Kaluga to Vilna, inflicting minimal combat casualties upon themselves during this maneuver.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f1e8fe38b90ff6dffdb7380ea9e159d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77045b1c2599a8ac0acb0e781562ae49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774750</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Advance Guard">
  <data key="d0">Advance Guard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Advance Guard is a tactical unit deployed ahead of the main body to secure routes for marching troops.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Outpost">
  <data key="d0">Outpost</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Outpost consists of distributed sentry lines designed to protect stationary forces during rest periods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three Column Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Three Column Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Three Column Deployment involves splitting a large force into three parallel segments which limits flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Winter Quarters">
  <data key="d0">Winter Quarters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Winter Quarters refer to extended stationary encampments protected by outpost lines due to harsh conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marching Forces">
  <data key="d0">Marching Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Marching Forces denote active troops in motion requiring advance guarding for security.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stationary Forces">
  <data key="d0">Stationary Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stationary Forces denote troops that have halted and require outpost-style protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1813 Campaign">
  <data key="d0">1813 Campaign</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The 1813 Campaign marks the period where Macdonald and Blucher operated against each other in Silesia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terrain Characteristics">
  <data key="d0">Terrain Characteristics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Terrain Characteristics dictate whether scouts or sentry lines are sufficient for protecting advancing troops.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Corps">
  <data key="d0">Army Corps</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An Army Corps is a military formation consisting of approximately 3 to 4 divisions used in strategic deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Major military units organized under commanders like Macdonald and Blücher, typically divided into三路(routes).&lt;SEP&gt;Army Corps are large military units consisting of several regiments, serving as a potential base for forming an advance guard.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57f8d4f60b7c38f053668dcb48030cc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Division">
  <data key="d0">Division</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Division is a significant subordinate military unit positioned within the army hierarchy, specifically defined as a subdivision of a Legion or Corps. In terms of relative scale, the Division is recognized as smaller than a Corps but larger than Battalions, serving as a foundational element that composes larger formations such as Army Corps in strategic calculations. Within these higher-level structures, the Division acts as the direct operational component in certain hierarchies and is typically divided further into Brigades, often consisting of five specific brigades.

Regarding physical composition and manning, descriptions vary regarding the exact number of personnel involved. Some accounts characterize a Division as a military formation typically sized between 10,000 to 12,000 men used primarily in defensive roles, while other data indicates the unit is typically composed of approximately 8,000 soldiers. Regardless of the specific headcount variance, the unit is noted as being subject to specific marching constraints. Functionally, Divisions possess operational autonomy; they are sub-units capable of acting independently or serving as reserves during marches, contingent upon the overall army structure.

Tactically, the role of the Division evolves based on command strategy. Depending on the army structure, they are capable of composing an advance guard. Additionally, provided historical data suggests that for a Division, a military unit whose arrival order became less critical under the new battle sequence. These attributes collectively define the Division's versatility as a self-contained force ranging from defensive stances to reserve capacities within complex military operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-58e69b3684e2bdfaee22cb77833e8174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93ec7c5511e20868c576f5b6c5fefe41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38f13e59336c7bb333fab6bbbe0ac097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a0950d107000f63848660e8ffc0706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb8178aa22ecf62d882629768387c8db</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sentinel Line">
  <data key="d0">Sentinel Line</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
