<node id="Frederick the Great">
  <data key="d0">Frederick the Great</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Frederick the Great was a Prussian monarch and military commander renowned for his strategic acumen during the 18th century. He combined brilliant striking action with cautious restraint, utilizing rapid movement and minimal outposts to lead armies typically ranging between 30,000 and 40,000 men. His campaigns frequently involved high-speed marches across regions such as Saxony, Silesia, and Berlin, allowing him to leverage mobility effectively. To sustain these forces while maintaining fiscal prudence, he relied on an austere supply system and often employed foreign forces to maintain army sizes.

In terms of military innovation, Frederick the Great is associated with establishing horse artillery during the 18th century. During the later years of the Seven Years War, he notably increased artillery usage to achieve parity against stronger opponents. Despite logistical challenges inherent in his austere supply system, he achieved significant results through calculated strategies. For instance, in early 1762, he stayed near Breslau, utilizing specific camping strategies that contributed to his operational successes throughout his reign over Prussia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68bce0e3733aefaac111940411ed6dd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grawert">
  <data key="d0">Grawert</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Grawert was a Prussian military theorist whose work emphasized the critical importance of adapting military operations to terrain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Massenbach">
  <data key="d0">Massenbach</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Massenbach was a Prussian military theorist known for advocating views similar to Lloyd regarding the significance of terrain knowledge in warfare.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Destructive Firepower">
  <data key="d0">Destructive Firepower</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Destructive Firepower is identified as one of the two fundamental components of combat, utilized primarily by artillery and infantry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Close Combat">
  <data key="d0">Close Combat</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Close Combat refers to the second component of battle involving single-combat or bayonet fighting elements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absolute Offense">
  <data key="d0">Absolute Offense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absolute Offense is defined within the tactical framework as the aspect of movement and attack without static defensive holding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absolute Defense">
  <data key="d0">Absolute Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absolute Defense is defined as the static element of combat likened to trees rooted in the ground for holding positions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter Four Proportions">
  <data key="d0">Chapter Four Proportions</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter Four discusses the ratios and roles of different military branches including infantry, cavalry, and artillery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seven Years War">
  <data key="d0">Seven Years War</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Seven Years War is a historical conflict where Frederick the Great adopted strategies such as increasing artillery presence to balance enemy strength.&lt;SEP&gt;The Seven Years War is a historical conflict cited to illustrate past difficulties in march organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a0950d107000f63848660e8ffc0706</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Middle Ages">
  <data key="d0">Middle Ages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Middle Ages is a historical period where cavalry was perceived as disproportionately important compared to infantry despite lower numerical proportions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crusades">
  <data key="d0">Crusades</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Crusades involved large amounts of infantry, challenging the perception that medieval armies consisted almost entirely of cavalry.&lt;SEP&gt;The Crusades were eight expeditions led by Western lords and merchants to conquer Eastern territories between 1096 and 1291.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holy Roman Emperor">
  <data key="d0">Holy Roman Emperor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Holy Roman Emperor led expeditions to Rome supported by significant infantry forces, contrary to assumptions about medieval cavalry dominance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rome">
  <data key="d0">Rome</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Rome is a location targeted by the Holy Roman Emperor during historical expeditions involving large infantry detachments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Germany">
  <data key="d0">Germany</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Germany was a site of smaller military conflicts in certain periods where small armies composed purely of cavalry were more common.&lt;SEP&gt;Germany is the modern nation associated with Leipzig and the Saxony state mentioned in the footnotes.&lt;SEP&gt;Germany is a European country traversed by the Danube River and hosting cities such as Cologne.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774763</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Italy">
  <data key="d0">Italy</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Italy was involved in local conflicts during periods where the prevalence of pure cavalry armies was higher than modern times.&lt;SEP&gt;Italy received 43 expeditions from the Holy Roman Empire between 951 and 1250.&lt;SEP&gt;Italy is a European country containing Upper Italy and bordered by the Alps and Apennine Mountains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774763</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thirty Years War">
  <data key="d0">Thirty Years War</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Thirty Years War marks a transition period where reliance on infantry decreased due to shifts toward mercenary systems and monetary recruitment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774763</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="People's War">
  <data key="d0">People's War</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">People's Wars rely on reserve and national guard forces, necessitating large infantry formations due to equipment scarcity compared to personnel needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774764</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reserve Army">
  <data key="d0">Reserve Army</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reserve Armies and National Guards serve as essential components in mobilizing large numbers of infantry for Peoples' Wars lacking heavy equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Reserve Army was a military force funded and supplied by provincial governments, abolished in 1859.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774764</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Offense">
  <data key="d0">Offense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Offense describes a military action mode where the attacker may still exhibit caution and deliberation depending on the broader strategic context.&lt;SEP&gt;Offense is a strategic mode where the advantages of high ground can still be utilized through temporary defensive pauses during marches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774764</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defense">
  <data key="d0">Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The essence of defensive action characterized by holding position firmly like a tree rooted in the ground.&lt;SEP&gt;Defense describes a military action mode where defenders may also act boldly and aggressively, blurring distinctions from offensive postures.&lt;SEP&gt;Defense is a strategic mode where occupying high ground provides superior security and difficulty for the enemy to approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774765</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National Guard">
  <data key="d0">National Guard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">National Guard is a type of auxiliary force utilized alongside reserve armies in People's Wars, often formed due to equipment shortages relative to personnel.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774765</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weaker Army">
  <data key="d0">Weaker Army</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Weaker Army describes a military force with fewer resources or personnel that employs strategies like increased artillery to achieve equilibrium against stronger opponents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774765</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feudal Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Feudal Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Feudal Obligation refers to historical military service duties that were abolished in favor of mercenary systems during the transition period of the Thirty Years War.&lt;SEP&gt;Feudal Obligation described duties such as taxation and military service owed by vassals to kings or emperors in medieval Europe.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3c6b62745fd7ff550d76846c1a46a8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774765</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cavalry Squadron">
  <data key="d0">Cavalry Squadron</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A cavalry unit characterized by approximately 150 warhorses and significant mobility capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774766</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infantry Battalion">
  <data key="d0">Infantry Battalion</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An infantry unit composed of 800 soldiers, serving as the numerical core of the fighting force.&lt;SEP&gt;A standard infantry unit, defined historically as comprising approximately 800 personnel for comparative analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental infantry sub-unit established by regular peacetime编制used as a basis for constructing larger divisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific military unit, such as a separate infantry company, that stations in camps.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6fa33c8a5d89283faf658b97761a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774766</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artillery Battery">
  <data key="d0">Artillery Battery</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An artillery unit equipped with eight 6-pound guns, providing heavy firepower but limited mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized artillery sub-unit integrated into the standard divisional structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774766</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Narrow Military Art">
  <data key="d0">Narrow Military Art</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The theoretical discipline focusing on the effective deployment of existing armies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774766</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combat Cost">
  <data key="d0">Combat Cost</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A quantifiable metric involving expenses used to compare the relative value of different military branches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Firepower">
  <data key="d0">Firepower</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The destructive force exerted by troops, significantly increased by the presence of artillery.&lt;SEP&gt;The destructive principle of weaponry that plays a major role in modern warfare, particularly associated with artillery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mobility">
  <data key="d0">Mobility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The degree of speed and flexibility available for troop maneuvers, heavily dependent on cavalry strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774768</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artillery Gun">
  <data key="d0">Artillery Gun</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Heavy weaponry utilized by artillery units that requires constant supply and protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774768</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ammunition Wagon">
  <data key="d0">Ammunition Wagon</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Vehicles used to transport ammunition, critical for artillery operations yet vulnerable to enemy capture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774768</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warhorse">
  <data key="d0">Warhorse</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Animals ridden or harnessed by cavalry units to facilitate rapid movement and combat actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774769</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defensive Posture">
  <data key="d0">Defensive Posture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategic state adopted when artillery forces are abundant, emphasizing fortification and passive defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774769</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Offensive Posture">
  <data key="d0">Offensive Posture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategic state adopted during artillery scarcity, favoring active and aggressive tactics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774769</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terrain Obstacle">
  <data key="d0">Terrain Obstacle</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Geographical barriers such as mountains used to shield large concentrations of artillery from enemy advance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774770</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Logistical Burden">
  <data key="d0">Supply Logistical Burden</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The strain on provisions and resources caused by maintaining a disproportionately large number of cavalry units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e041dafef58e97722effb6d5aec49ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774770</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Austria">
  <data key="d0">Austria</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Austria is a Central European nation-state located along the course of the Danube River, containing specific regions like Styria. Throughout its history, the country has been defined by significant geopolitical rivalries and military engagements. Notably, Austria contested Silesia with Prussia during the Silesian Wars, positioning itself as a nation opposing Prussian interests in those conflicts. Diplomatic and military cooperation was also evident when the nation sent troops under the command of Lacy to support the Russian Army in 1760. Later, during the Napoleonic era, Austria participated in the Seventh Coalition and contributed the Main Army under Schwarzenberg.

Domestically, Austria developed distinct military organizational features, having established Landwehr units and hosting the appearance of the Landsturm militia. The military also utilized specific transport vehicles for artillery operations. Additionally, Austria is identified as a nation retaining remnants of Tatar systems in its military and administrative structure. These elements collectively illustrate Austria's role as a multifaceted European power engaging in various wars while maintaining unique administrative and military characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea6ac7b39b9305030f2c7226a86db43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ed0edc361ac37e0deef9374d8ec557e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f00c9046ba6740391ca82c677577b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7f6e47322107075587da5b8dd1c7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
