  <data key="d3">chunk-a6fa33c8a5d89283faf658b97761a915</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Time Constraints">
  <data key="d0">Operational Time Constraints</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational Time Constraints refer to the lack of sufficient time available for troops to implement slower, more organized supply gathering methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6fa33c8a5d89283faf658b97761a915</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enemy Front">
  <data key="d0">Enemy Front</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Enemy Front denotes the territory directly in contact with opposing forces where immediate supply action is critical.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6fa33c8a5d89283faf658b97761a915</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mountain">
  <data key="d0">Mountain</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Mountain terrain is characterized by restricted movement and elevation advantages that slow down force operations significantly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Woodland">
  <data key="d0">Woodland</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Woodland terrain primarily obstructs line of sight and prevents monitoring, complicating coordination during combat.&lt;SEP&gt;A Woodland refers to forested areas located below troop positions that can obstruct the ability to observe enemy movements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deep Ploughed Land">
  <data key="d0">Deep Ploughed Land</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Deep ploughed land features ditches, fences, hedges, and earthworks that create obstacles affecting military movement and tactics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soldier">
  <data key="d0">Soldier</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Soldiers assume greater responsibility for tactical results and individual initiative when operating in dispersed formations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warfare">
  <data key="d0">Warfare</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Warfare encompasses combat operations where terrain dictates complexity, technique requirements, and command structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regular Army">
  <data key="d0">Regular Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Regular Army adapts to national defense needs by acquiring traits suitable for dispersed combat scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="People's Armed Forces">
  <data key="d0">People's Armed Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">People's Armed Forces leverage high morale to gain advantages in complex, dispersed terrains despite lacking large-scale cohesion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Troop Dispersion">
  <data key="d0">Troop Dispersion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Troop Dispersion is the division of forces into smaller units, often necessitated by complex terrain and increasing individual autonomy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774776</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Swamp">
  <data key="d0">Swamp</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Swamp terrain is identified as one of the three main terrains impacting warfare complexity and technique requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Ground">
  <data key="d0">High Ground</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">High Ground is a strategic position offering advantages in observation, firing accuracy, and movement control in warfare.&lt;SEP&gt;High Ground refers to elevated positions within mountainous areas that allow commanders to observe and control other locations.&lt;SEP&gt;High Ground refers to elevated terrain that provides strategic advantages such as observation and defensive superiority.&lt;SEP&gt;High Ground is a strategic elevation that offers control advantages and can become true control depending on its possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee5ef82512a6fb9049bd1bfe1863bb38</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispersed Line">
  <data key="d0">Dispersed Line</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A Dispersed Line describes a tactical formation where a large army spreads into a long line, forcing independent engagement by individual soldiers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-adb948420cdd6d2bd78179f476f0df5a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Zieten">
  <data key="d0">General Zieten</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Prussian commander who led the First Corps in defense against Napoleon's forces at Ligny.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prussian First Corps">
  <data key="d0">Prussian First Corps</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A military unit consisting of approximately 30,000 men under the command of General Zieten.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battle of Ligny">
  <data key="d0">Battle of Ligny</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A military engagement occurring between June 15 and 16 where Zieten's forces resisted Napoleon.&lt;SEP&gt;The Battle of Ligny is a military engagement where Zieten participated alongside other historical events.&lt;SEP&gt;The Battle of Ligny is the engagement where Napoleon planned to attack after 2 PM on June 16.&lt;SEP&gt;The Battle of Ligny is a military offensive characterized by caution due to the threat of dual opponents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ligny">
  <data key="d0">Ligny</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The destination location of the march where the significant battle against Zieten took place.&lt;SEP&gt;Ligny is a historical site now part of Sombreffe, Belgium, associated with the Battle of Ligny.&lt;SEP&gt;Ligny is attacked by Gerard's 4th Army and forms part of the Prussian defensive line.&lt;SEP&gt;Ligny is a location identified in the text as the site of an offensive launch mentioned in the opening paragraph.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774778</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Charleroi">
  <data key="d0">Charleroi</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The starting location of the short distance march of less than 2 lieues to Ligny.&lt;SEP&gt;Charleroi is a modern city in Belgium located on the Sambre River, north of Brussels.&lt;SEP&gt;Charleroi is occupied by Napoleon after repelling Zieten's vanguard.&lt;SEP&gt;Charleroi is the location near which General Ziethen commanded the vanguard force of the Prussian Army.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5bb89666881fe685b53314cebe1abaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea</data>
