</node>
<node id="Requisition Teams">
  <data key="d0">Requisition Teams</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Requisition Teams are assigned power to enforce collection alongside local authorities to secure the majority of allocated grain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Factor">
  <data key="d0">Time Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Time Factor determines the success of Regular Requisition, with more time allowing wider distribution and lighter burdens on the populace.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Area">
  <data key="d0">Supply Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supply Area expands daily around the campsite, theoretically growing from 4 square prys to significantly larger regions over consecutive days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="French Revolution">
  <data key="d0">French Revolution</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The French Revolution began in 1789 and established the French Republic, leading to subsequent wars.&lt;SEP&gt;The French Revolution disrupted the existing war system, leading to the collapse of complex supply structures.&lt;SEP&gt;French Revolution provides historical evidence supporting the viability of acquiring supplies immediately upon arrival for large armies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8520f94840f0c06aabc2e77297761e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eighteenth Century Warfare">
  <data key="d0">Eighteenth Century Warfare</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Eighteenth Century Warfare illustrates a distinct provisioning style where armies solved logistics independently without government aid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cash Purchase">
  <data key="d0">Cash Purchase</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cash Purchase serves as an auxiliary tool to Regular Requisition, feasible when sufficient time and resources exist.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774790</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supplies">
  <data key="d0">Supplies</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplies encompass ammunition, baggage, and logistical goods essential for troop operations, subject to capture or loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Supplies refer to essential food and materials required for military sustainment, obtained through requisition or purchase mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Supplies refer to provisions such as food and equipment stored in bases, distinguishable between locally obtainable items and imports from home regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Supplies include weapons and ammunition that require secure storage in fortresses or distant locations rather than near the front line.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77045b1c2599a8ac0acb0e781562ae49</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baggage Train">
  <data key="d0">Baggage Train</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Large logistical units that hinder combat operations, typically reduced in size except during exceptional circumstances.&lt;SEP&gt;A large logistical convoy consisting of transport wagons that traditionally hindered combat operations by occupying space and resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Front Guard">
  <data key="d0">Front Guard</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The vanguard unit of an army that arrives ahead of the main force, facing extreme time constraints for securing local provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moscow">
  <data key="d0">Moscow</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Moscow is the capital of Russia and serves as a pivotal entity within the context of the 1812 military campaign. Strategically, it functioned as the primary destination for the Russian Army following battles near Smolensk when they were forced to retreat. Simultaneously, Moscow represented the ultimate objective of the French advance, reached after sustaining heavy losses before being captured by French forces. Following this occupation, the city marks the starting point of the French retreat considered part of the broader 1812 campaign actions, specifically identified as the location from which Napoleon's forces retreated before winter set in.

Geographically and historically, Moscow is defined by its proximity to critical engagements. It is a major city near which the Battle of Borodino took place prior to its capture, while the Battle of Tarutino also occurred near the city in the context of the Russian campaign. Additionally, the city acts as an endpoint of a supply route that becomes increasingly difficult to provision over distance due to the extent of the lines. These combined elements highlight Moscow’s role as a central geographic and strategic focal point during the conflict involving the French invasion and subsequent withdrawal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9577d681c513d8a72e8f9e09ad316f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher Authorities">
  <data key="d0">Higher Authorities</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Elevated administrative bodies responsible for overseeing supply arrangement and ensuring equitable burden distribution during prolonged occupations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e30125fa729c9d2fb7970405171875</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tottleben">
  <data key="d0">Tottleben</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Tottleben commanded the Russian army during the advance toward Prussian capital Berlin in autumn 1760.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lacy">
  <data key="d0">Lacy</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lacy was an Austrian commander who led 18,000 men from Silesia to support the Russian forces.&lt;SEP&gt;An Austrian commander dispatched with troops to support the Russian Army in 1760.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774791</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Daun">
  <data key="d0">Daun</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Daun was an opposing commander against whom Frederick the Great conducted operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Daun is the Austrian Marshal referenced as having defeated Friedrich II at the Battle of Hochkirch in 1758.&lt;SEP&gt;Daun served as the Austrian commander who intervened near Olmütz to rescue the trapped Austrian forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68bce0e3733aefaac111940411ed6dd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ceef5e0b237a9edd2580a6c4fbfed04</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Berlin">
  <data key="d0">Berlin</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Berlin is the location where the Northern Army of the Sixth Coalition was deployed.&lt;SEP&gt;Berlin was the capital of Prussia and the strategic target of the Russian army's advance.&lt;SEP&gt;A major destination reached by Frederick the Great's forces in 1760 during a rapid march covering 45 leagues in 10 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oder River">
  <data key="d0">Oder River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Oder River served as a key route for the Russian army marching toward Berlin.&lt;SEP&gt;A river route utilized by the Russian Army during their 1760 advance towards Berlin.&lt;SEP&gt;A major European river that serves as a tributary source for the Katzbach stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Schweidnitz">
  <data key="d0">Schweidnitz</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Schweidnitz was a town in Silesia, later known as Swidnica, from which Lacy began his march.&lt;SEP&gt;A location serving as the starting point for Frederick the Great's rapid march to Berlin in 1760.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tempelhof Village">
  <data key="d0">Tempelhof Village</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Tempelhof Village was a settlement south of Berlin where Austrian forces arrived in early October.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breslau">
  <data key="d0">Breslau</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Breslau was a city located northeast of Schweidnitz in Silesia.&lt;SEP&gt;Breslau is the city near which Frederick the Great conducted similar troop camping operations in 1762.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moravia">
  <data key="d0">Moravia</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Moravia was the region invaded by the Prussian army under Frederick II in May 1758.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Olmütz">
  <data key="d0">Olmütz</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Olmütz was a fortified city in Moravia where Frederick II attempted to lay siege before retreating.&lt;SEP&gt;A city where Frederick the Great lifted a siege in 1758 to redirect his army for a different campaign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774792</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bohemia">
  <data key="d0">Bohemia</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Bohemia was the deployment area for the Main Army commanded by Schwarzenberg.&lt;SEP&gt;A region containing the city of Prague and serving as the setting for military maneuvers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bohemia was the destination of Frederick II's army retreat after abandoning the siege of Olmütz.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb8178aa22ecf62d882629768387c8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marching">
  <data key="d0">Marching</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Marching refers to the movement of troops along roads, involving separation of artillery and cavalry rotation.&lt;SEP&gt;Marching involves moving troops between deployment points, requiring comfort and accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Marching refers to the movement of troops, identified as a significant factor causing physical damage to armies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="War">
  <data key="d0">War</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">War serves as the overarching stage and context for military campaigns, marching, and resource consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;War is a state of armed conflict where logistical capabilities determine strategic direction and duration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc6725d3545cdb40e1dfdd2209aba40f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1e8fe38b90ff6dffdb7380ea9e159d7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicles">
  <data key="d0">Vehicles</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Vehicles refer to transport wagons or carts used by the army that are prone to damage and breakdown during rough marching conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horses">
  <data key="d0">Horses</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Horses are the animal mounts used by cavalry forces, susceptible to saddle injuries and exhaustion from continuous service.&lt;SEP&gt;Horses are sensitive to supply shortages, particularly starvation due to lack of fodder compared to human soldiers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Illness">
  <data key="d0">Illness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Illness refers to the health deterioration and disease outbreaks caused by harsh environmental conditions during military movements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Lines">
  <data key="d0">Supply Lines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supply Lines denote the logistical routes connecting troops to their bases, whose compromise forces strategic withdrawals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e5cd33cb23eede2329c705a877b7cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774793</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concentration Of Forces">
  <data key="d0">Concentration Of Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concentration Of Forces is a military principle where troops gather together to maximize strength, contrasted with dispersal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispersion Of Forces">
  <data key="d0">Dispersion Of Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dispersion Of Forces refers to spreading troops apart, often dictated by terrain or political characteristics, contrary to concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terrain Morphology">
  <data key="d0">Terrain Morphology</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Terrain Morphology describes the physical characteristics of land, such as mountains or ploughed fields, influencing military operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spain">
  <data key="d0">Spain</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A nation in Europe traversed by the Tajo river.&lt;SEP&gt;Spain is a location where Napoleon's forces faced difficulties due to resource depletion from the requisition method.&lt;SEP&gt;Spain was a major signatory party in the Peace of Westphalia negotiations, represented by the House of Habsburg.&lt;SEP&gt;Spain served as a theater where French troops were forced to disperse and locals utilized uprisings for defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc6725d3545cdb40e1dfdd2209aba40f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b03fbc1bc0a2f36813dfff78edd095</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cambrai">
  <data key="d0">Cambrai</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Cambrai is a location on the route facilitating the flow of daily rations to Paris without military force.&lt;SEP&gt;Cambrai is a city in the Nord department of France, historically noted as a fortress located on the Scheldt River.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adam Heinrich Dietrich Freiherr Von Bülow">
  <data key="d0">Adam Heinrich Dietrich Freiherr Von Bülow</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Adam Heinrich Dietrich Freiherr Von Bülow was a Prussian military theorist whose work on war theory and bases is referenced in notes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forest Areas">
  <data key="d0">Forest Areas</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Forest Areas are dense regions lacking road space for towing guns or feed for horses, making them unsuitable for artillery or cavalry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ploughed Fields">
  <data key="d0">Ploughed Fields</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ploughed Fields offer fewer disadvantages for artillery compared to deep ploughed areas but can still affect maneuverability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Control Position">
  <data key="d0">Control Position</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Control Position is a strategic concept aiming to secure dominance through key points or maneuvering capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strategic Maneuver">
  <data key="d0">Strategic Maneuver</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategic Maneuver involves moving forces effectively to gain advantageous positions relative to the enemy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Population Uprising">
  <data key="d0">Population Uprising</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Population Uprising is a form of national defense where civilians rise up, allowing Spanish forces to concentrate for battle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Military Theory">
  <data key="d0">Military Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Military Theory refers to the academic and practical study of warfare strategies, notably referenced in Adam Heinrich Dietrich Freiherr Von Bülow's works.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scheldt River">
  <data key="d0">Scheldt River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Scheldt River forms part of the lowland region historically known as the Netherlands.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scheldt River is a waterway flowing through central Belgium into which the Senne River discharges.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scheldt River is a prominent waterway located in northern Europe, passing through the city of Valenciennes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scheldt River is a geographical feature located near Cambrai, noted in historical footnotes as defining the city's position.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9b9839be4f5c76a97ace71baf8a2625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nord Department">
  <data key="d0">Nord Department</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Nord Department is an administrative department in the Hauts-de-France region of France, containing the city of Valenciennes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nord Department is a modern administrative region in northern France where the city of Cambrai is currently located.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movement Obstacle">
  <data key="d0">Movement Obstacle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movement Obstacle represents a strategic barrier provided by high ground that hinders the progress of opposing forces during combat.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attack">
  <data key="d0">Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The essence of offensive action characterized by movement and dynamic engagement against the enemy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Combat">
  <data key="d0">Single Combat</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Individual engagement considered the true independent foundation of battle alongside the principle of firepower.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff1b2820cc1e95701eebf7342c4385d7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774795</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Force Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The optimal proportional balance between different military branches required to achieve maximum combat power.</data>
