</node>
<node id="Neutral Zone">
  <data key="d0">Neutral Zone</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An area perceived as protective territory where enemies feel secure due to troop inflexibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a2918db8fd4527a62709762ff6d5e2b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silesian Wars">
  <data key="d0">Silesian Wars</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Silesian Wars mark the starting point for modern changes in the relationship between combat and non-combat states within armies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Silesian Wars (1740–1763) involved Prussia and allies versus Austria, France, Russia, and Sweden primarily over Silesia.&lt;SEP&gt;The Silesian Wars were a series of conflicts where Frederick the Great demonstrated superior talent despite occasional losses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68bce0e3733aefaac111940411ed6dd7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quartering">
  <data key="d0">Quartering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quartering is one of the three states of existence for armies outside of combat.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartering involves lodging in buildings, contrasting with camping.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartering refers to the military method of housing troops in buildings rather than tents, essential for modern military art.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d696492803499031b7e887c1814ba23</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Encampment">
  <data key="d0">Encampment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Encampment is one of the three states of existence for armies outside of combat alongside marching and quartering.&lt;SEP&gt;Designated areas for troops to stay, affecting logistical efficiency during long marches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb8178aa22ecf62d882629768387c8db</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Combat State">
  <data key="d0">Non-Combat State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-Combat State encompasses military activities such as quartering, marching, and encampment that occur outside direct fighting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combat">
  <data key="d0">Combat</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combat is distinguished from army studies; the chapter examines army aspects that enable combat rather than combat itself.&lt;SEP&gt;Combat represents the primary function of the army, historically seen as the blade while non-combat was merely the handle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9577d681c513d8a72e8f9e09ad316f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tactics">
  <data key="d0">Tactics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tactics governs the planning of battle formations and relates more to tactical rather than strategic scope.&lt;SEP&gt;Tactics governs small-scale troop movements and deployments, often overlapping significantly with strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tactics covers internal deployment within battles and the transition to combat status, distinct from strategic considerations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategy influences larger scale movements and interacts with battle formation at the point of converting distribution to combat deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Strategy operates on a larger time and space scale than tactics and influences the non-combat state.&lt;SEP&gt;Strategy involves examining army states like camps concerning location, time, and force size independent of immediate tactics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-223fc84be8831740cffa7582ec0ee05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a0950d107000f63848660e8ffc0706</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Army Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Army Deployment refers to the arrangement of forces, divided into general and special purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Deployment">
  <data key="d0">General Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">General Deployment is the overarching method applicable to encampment, quartering, and marching before specific goals are set.&lt;SEP&gt;General deployment refers to the disposition of military units before specific combat tasks are assigned, typically divided into parts separated by distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Special Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Deployment is determined by specific purposes and needs rather than general requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vanguard">
  <data key="d0">Vanguard</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A forward-deployed military unit positioned as the first line of engagement in an eight-part structure.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vanguard serves as a scout force deployed forward to observe enemy positions and terrain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93ec7c5511e20868c576f5b6c5fefe41</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Logistics">
  <data key="d0">Supply Logistics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acquiring supplies is a fundamental requirement for maintaining the army's existence during deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rear Security">
  <data key="d0">Rear Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rear Security ensures the safety of the army's flank and retreat routes during operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Open Ground">
  <data key="d0">Open Ground</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Open ground allows for easier observation and reconnaissance by vanguard and scouting forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Terrain">
  <data key="d0">Complex Terrain</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex terrain hinders observation and offers different defensive advantages compared to open ground.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strategic Support Point">
  <data key="d0">Strategic Support Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategic Support Points provide security without needing direct physical connection to the troop unit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tactical Support Point">
  <data key="d0">Tactical Support Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tactical Support Points are directly connected to the troops and cover a smaller range than strategic ones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coast">
  <data key="d0">Coast</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A coast acts as a strategic support point if the enemy cannot utilize the intervening space for maneuvering.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Great River">
  <data key="d0">Great River</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A great river can serve as a strategic support point preventing enemy deep flanking movements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lake">
  <data key="d0">Lake</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Lakes may serve as strategic obstacles or points depending on the length of the shoreline relative to operational ranges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lines of Communication">
  <data key="d0">Lines of Communication</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The chapter on Lines of Communication deals with the principles of rear security and retreat routes.&lt;SEP&gt;Routes that restrict retreating armies when supply conditions prevent maneuvering off established paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089e96fcb6c53d4fe37413f70873aedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elbe River">
  <data key="d0">Elbe River</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Elbe River is a prominent water body flowing through regions near Prague and the Izera River. Within Germany, the river is situated where the city of Kemberg is located, and it serves as a notable landmark near Wartenburg. Beyond its geographical definition, the Elbe River holds significant historical relevance regarding military campaigns. It marks a pivotal point during the French retreat after the Moscow campaign. Specifically, the French retreat crossed the Elbe River as part of the withdrawal beginning from Moscow. By March, French forces retreated to the west bank of the Elbe River, concluding the 1812 war situation.

Additionally, the Elbe River is associated with Blücher's strategic movements after the initial engagement in Silesia, serving as the destination Blücher targeted following these engagements. Both the French retreat and the strategic actions involving Blücher highlight the river's significance as a major geographical and tactical feature in European history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b7b86e9f8571611dae96128b88d77c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57f8d4f60b7c38f053668dcb48030cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d696492803499031b7e887c1814ba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9577d681c513d8a72e8f9e09ad316f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb8178aa22ecf62d882629768387c8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5caa7084b9aab1683b075dc4fc333250</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scout Units">
  <data key="d0">Scout Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Scout Units include spies and small forward detachments serving as strategic eyes for observation in the field.</data>
