  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1812 Russian Campaign">
  <data key="d0">1812 Russian Campaign</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The 1812 Russian Campaign was a military operation where the French Army operated for six months with little quartering under harsh climate.&lt;SEP&gt;A military operation where extreme conditions forced the use of large baggage trains despite general efficiency rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d696492803499031b7e887c1814ba23</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Site Requisitioning">
  <data key="d0">On-Site Requisitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A supply system based on utilizing local transport and bakeries, considered the foundation for army provisioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poland">
  <data key="d0">Poland</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Poland is the modern location of the former Passarge River.&lt;SEP&gt;A country containing historical cities like Goldberg and Löwenberg mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A country traversed by armies during the 1812 campaign involving vast distances and supply line challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Poland is a region compared to Flanders, noted for being less capable of supplying large armies due to lower population density.&lt;SEP&gt;Poland is the country where the Vistula River originates and is the longest river in the nation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc6725d3545cdb40e1dfdd2209aba40f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9b9839be4f5c76a97ace71baf8a2625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rhineland">
  <data key="d0">Rhineland</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Region cited as an example where prolonged military presence required sustainable supply arrangements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Saxony">
  <data key="d0">Saxony</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A region frequently traversed by Frederick the Great's forces in conjunction with Silesia during his campaigns.&lt;SEP&gt;The region associated with the 1813 campaign involving the York Corps and major engagements.&lt;SEP&gt;Saxony is a state within Germany noted as the location of the city of Leipzig.&lt;SEP&gt;Region cited as an example where large-scale troop concentrations occurred over extended war periods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blücher">
  <data key="d0">Blücher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Blücher is a distinguished historical military commander and Field Marshal known for his leadership of Prussian forces during the early 19th century. During the 1813 campaign, he served as a general operating in Silesia and Saxony, where he was noted for utilizing maneuvering warfare. In this theater, he encountered General Macdonald before subsequently moving his forces towards the Elbe River. The following year, during the 1814 campaigns, Blücher led Prussian forces that retreated across rivers while facing opposition.

By 1815, Blücher commanded a corps in the same theater as Wellington, creating an asymmetric force situation against the French. His field marshalate saw him deploy three corps along the line of Saint-Amand, Ligny, and Sombreffe. During the 1815 campaign near Charleroi, his vanguard was specifically led by General Ziethen. Following the engagements, Blücher played a crucial role in pursuing the French army, covering approximately 30 leagues in 10 days to ensure continued pressure. Throughout these conflicts, Blücher maintained a pivotal partnership with Wellington to coordinate coalition efforts against French dominance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-942da2473ae7c7645f080c44aba00371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8b814c286eb1e073648265d5177eb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66ab621011ad683c952d4d1bfeab457d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57f8d4f60b7c38f053668dcb48030cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9577d681c513d8a72e8f9e09ad316f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfca00a9897a7d585d9175d5bc52771c</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Upper Italy">
  <data key="d0">Upper Italy</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Region cited as an example concerning the sustainability of supply sources during multi-year conflicts.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper Italy, also known as Northern Italy, is a resource-rich inland region north of the Apennines including the Alps and Po Valley.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22b58faf893c88031c709a0cfac971fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9de7adb6e29e9aaaa3a95674b64a2552</data>
