  <data key="d0">Potsdam</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Potsdam is the German city where Jean de Beaurain's historical work was published in German translation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hainaut Province">
  <data key="d0">Hainaut Province</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Hainaut Province in Belgium contains historical battlefields including Fleurus and Steinkerque.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bace62a86e707c9881144ea59dde7a4a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Upper Forces">
  <data key="d0">Upper Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Upper Forces are troops occupying higher positions, experiencing feelings of security and superiority due to their location.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Forces">
  <data key="d0">Lower Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Lower Forces are troops positioned at lower elevations, perceiving disadvantages and insecurity relative to upper forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enemy">
  <data key="d0">Enemy</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The presence of nearby enemies is a primary factor preventing the use of quartering positions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Enemy is the adversarial force whose movements and intentions dictate defensive postures and the size of the encampment zone.&lt;SEP&gt;The Enemy represents the opposing force facing the upper forces, whose approach and positioning are constrained by the high ground.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d696492803499031b7e887c1814ba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15f89854b5dda629cc5dcfc4edcef1c4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battle of Hohenfriedberg">
  <data key="d0">Battle of Hohenfriedberg</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Battle of Hohenfriedberg is cited as an example where forces moved from a mountain to a plain, affecting the utility of high ground advantages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mountain Position">
  <data key="d0">Mountain Position</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A Mountain Position is a specific location on a mountain or slope where defenders gain control through high ground elevation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774804</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="River Valley">
  <data key="d0">River Valley</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A River Valley is a geographical feature mentioned as a constraint on movement if high ground is not occupied on both sides.&lt;SEP&gt;River Valley is a terrain feature that requires occupation of heights on both sides to be successfully defended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee5ef82512a6fb9049bd1bfe1863bb38</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maps">
  <data key="d0">Maps</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Maps are tools used to select commanding positions, though practical findings on the ground often contradict map-based selections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Imagination">
  <data key="d0">Imagination</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Imagination is described as a factor that amplifies the psychological effect of high ground beyond actual physical conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter Mountain Defense">
  <data key="d0">Chapter Mountain Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter Mountain Defense is a referenced section that elaborates on how defenses utilize high ground in mountainous areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Observation">
  <data key="d0">Observation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Observation refers to the ability to see enemy movements and terrain from elevated positions, granting strategic information advantages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-407ccaa628a42045b9ed85ea7bc4db1d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plain">
  <data key="d0">Plain</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A Plain is a flat geographical area where high ground advantages may be negated during engagements like the Battle of Hohenfriedberg.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Detached Units">
  <data key="d0">Detached Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Detached Units are small military forces dispatched from high ground positions to exert control and increase overall force effectiveness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geographic Slope">
  <data key="d0">Geographic Slope</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A Geographic Slope represents a larger strategic region, such as a province, acting as a continuous elevation for controlling front areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a37fce5a6357434f64db4fb518e01be0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="French Central Army">
  <data key="d0">French Central Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The primary military force of Napoleon in 1812, starting with approximately 301,000 men and suffering massive attrition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smolensk">
  <data key="d0">Smolensk</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A city near which Napoleon dispatched troops and where the army sustained further losses before reaching Moscow.&lt;SEP&gt;A western Russian city located on the Dnieper River near the Belarus border during the 1812 Campaign.&lt;SEP&gt;A site of three days of battle where the Russian Army continued to retreat following the engagement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Davout">
  <data key="d0">Davout</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A French commander sent by Napoleon to advance towards Mogilev and engage Bagration's Russian forces on July 23.&lt;SEP&gt;A marshal leading one of the key French armies engaged against the Russians at early stages of the campaign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bagration">
  <data key="d0">Bagration</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Russian commander who fought against Davout in a notable engagement prior to the fall of Moscow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Murat">
  <data key="d0">Murat</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Murat commanded forces at the Battle of Tarutino and missed an opportunity to utilize outpost lines effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Murat is a military commander who led the French army in the Battle of Tarutino and later retreated after losses.&lt;SEP&gt;A marshal who fought against Ostermann-Tolstoy in a significant battle before the advance on Moscow.&lt;SEP&gt;A French commander whose forces encountered Russian vanguard troops at Ostrovno in late July during the 1812 Campaign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd9ee2ca6a2dbeb008f986d03860045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ceef5e0b237a9edd2580a6c4fbfed04</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ostermann-Tolstoy">
  <data key="d0">Ostermann-Tolstoy</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Russian general who opposed Murat in a battle during the 1812 campaign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vilna">
  <data key="d0">Vilna</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The city where the pursuing Russian army arrived after chasing the retreating French from Moscow.&lt;SEP&gt;Vilna is a starting point of a military route noted for severe supply difficulties requiring force to obtain food.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2a90ee6c570c1f41213848bde0e55e5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vitebsk">
  <data key="d0">Vitebsk</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A location where the French army halted for rest during the advance towards Moscow.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitebsk is the city near which Napoleon conducted similar troop camping operations in 1812.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adfddc40b68714749a17f1f73f04abea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kaluga">
  <data key="d0">Kaluga</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The region from which the Russian army began its pursuit of the retreating French forces.&lt;SEP&gt;The capital of Kaluga Oblast in Russia, located north of Borodino on the Oka River.&lt;SEP&gt;A location suggested as an alternative retreat direction to mislead the enemy away from Moscow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72c55a94307c751d0e9fbf6270e7773d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c6e328b37fa2162a0fbd3f638331548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516f6a00a475faea10c611a860305297</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\战争论.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778774806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
